{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1303641-expanded-rules-for-multiple-personalities","id":1303641,"title":"Expanded Rules for Multiple Personalities.","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2019-04-08T16:12:46-07:00","id":1303641,"title":"Expanded Rules for Multiple Personalities.","content":"Multiple Personality Disorder (MPD)<br /> <br /> In what might be seen as an advanced fugue state, a person with MPD consciously and/or subconsciously constructs one or more different identities. Each identity can become quite distinct, with its own speech patterns, memories, behaviors, and even physical appearance. Also called dissociative identity disorder (DID), MPD could manifest anywhere from two separate identities to a dozen or more\u2026 especially when it\u2019s augmented with Life, Mind, Correspondence, or a mixture of the three. MPD can be extremely common among Marauders, but it\u2019s frighteningly common among other mages too\u2026 especially if a mage and his Avatar begin to switch places or merge identities.<br /> <br /> This condition often has its origins in abuse; the dissociation is a form of self-defense. After all, it would be much better if these terrible things had happened to someone else... the formation of a second personality may occur during childhood to cope with psychological damage.<br /> <br /> If this trait is taken by a player, then it should be discussed which personality is dominant, how often the alternate personality (or personalities) emerge, and whether the storyteller or the player are in control when that happens. An alternate personality could be a rare manifestation that only comes up when the character is placed under serious emotional strain, or they could be a constant visitor who regularly swaps places with the main personality. All personalities should have their own ratings in certain traits, their own Backgrounds, and perhaps their own Abilities; if this does happen, use the following system:<br /> <br /> Each Personality has a Charisma + Manipulation equal to that of the main personality, but the way the dots are divided may vary. The friendly Anne (Charisma 4, Manipulation 1) may give way to the stoic and convincing Jess (Charisma 2, Manipulation 3). The same applies to the Mental Attributes. If the source of the personalities is supernatural, then this could even apply, at storyteller discretion, to Physical Attributes. Jason is a flexible and dexterous twink (Strength 1, Dexterity 5, Stamina 4) but when Jack comes out to play he\u2019s far stronger than he looks (Strength 5, Dexterity 3, Stamina 2).<br /> <br /> Dots in Abilities are segregated between the different personalities in some cases, and shared in others. Maybe Anne and Jess share all of their abilities with few exceptions; Anne never learned to fight (0 in Brawl) but she certainly learned to hide (4 in Stealth). The reverse is true with Jess, who has 3 in Brawl and 0 in Stealth. These dots are not free, and the choice to segregate them between personalities reflects the drawbacks of having two separate people learning and growing in a single body with limited time and experience. A player should choose a few examples like this that suit the purpose of the dissociative personality; in Anne\u2019s case Jess would come out when hiding from bullies and dangerous relatives didn\u2019t work, to bite and kick her way out of trouble. Expanding on these ideas, Jess has Streetwise and Intimidation while Anne knows Law and Politics.<br /> <br /> A Personality may be taken as an Enemy if the player wishes; in that case they may take the relevant flaw and enjoy the carnage. The default state is that the two personalities are ambivalent toward one another, and generally work together to remain safe and healthy. A vicious or dangerous alternate personality is a much more dangerous flaw; just ask Hyde! And the reverse can be true for a Nephandus or dark Mage of course... imagine how horrible it must be to have a fucking would-be angel knocking around inside your head, taking over whenever it suits him...<br /> <br /> Backgrounds may be segregated also; Anne knows the PINs and bank details (Resources 2), but Jess is the one who knows who to call when she\u2019s in trouble (Contacts 2). This comes from Anne holding down a job and Jess wandering around town looking to make friends. Backgrounds like Avatar are always shared.<br /> <br /> Whether each personality is Awakened, or just the dominant one, will vary. Arete ratings may even vary. To avoid abuse, assume that an alternate personality has an Arete equal to or lesser than the main personality, unless they\u2019re an antagonist or mysterious figure under storyteller control. Furthermore, dots in Spheres should either be divided between the two personalities (2 in Mind and 1 in Prime for Anne, 2 in Forces and 1 in Life for Jess) or shared entirely. A player doesn\u2019t get multiple sets of starting Spheres to toy with.<br /> <br /> Different personalities have their own relationships, and this can create a great deal of drama. Richard owes his life to Jess, but knows he can\u2019t trust Anne... and yet he can\u2019t even be sure that these self-proclaimed \u201cidentical twins\u201d are really two people...<br /> <br /> Communicating with your own inner personalities directly requires a simple Mind 1 effect if the storyteller believes that it\u2019s just another part of your own Mind pattern, or a Mind 2 effect if the storyteller believes it to be a separate Mind pattern sharing the same Life pattern. This can also be achieved with Meditation and Demesne rolls. A Mind 1 effect can temporarily empower a Mage\u2019s mind, giving them the Willpower and clarity needed to stay in control. A Mind 4 Wonder can repress other personalities entirely as long as it is worn or used (and you can expect them to return with a vengeance when the item\u2019s effects are finally removed).<br /> <br /> Certain Merits and Flaws may be limited to one personality; John has the Green Thumb, while his repressed personality has the Dark Triad merit. The segregation will not always be complete; John is a local hero, but since his 'dark passenger' looks exactly like him, he may exploit that fame and the faith of the community.<br /> <br /> Struggles between the two personalities are resolved using Willpower rolls, difficulty equal to the temporary Willpower of the opposing personality. The personality that has more to lose may gain a bonus in this struggle, of -1 to -3 difficulty.<br /> <br /> When this condition develops in a Mage it can have supernatural origins; ghosts, past lives, your avatar, deliberate fracturing inflicted with Mind... but more commonly it is a natural phenomenon.<br /> <br /> For good FICTIONAL inspiration for this Derangement, I'd recommend Heroes (Season One), Vampire the Masquerade: Bloodlines and Clocktower ll The Struggle Within (released as Clocktower; Ghost Head in Japan... it's technically the third Clocktower game, so don't get it mixed up with Clocktower, which was Clocktower ll in Japan, since Clocktower the First Fear was never released in the west; regardless, you absolutely shouldn't PLAY Clocktower ll The Struggle Within, since the game is horribly designed. The story is interesting, however, telling the tale of a kind girl with a violent and aggressive man named Bates that manifests when she is in danger, fighting back against the Slasher Pursuers that are faced throughout the game). Each story features an example of this sort of internal relationship at play, and the first and last case show how these alternate personalities can interact with supernatural abilities.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 04-08-2019, 07:17 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-09T05:41:53-07:00","id":1303758,"title":"","content":"Just a point that you seem to have fallen for the <a href=\"https://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/SplitPersonality\" target=\"_blank\">Hollywood Multiple Personality Meme</a>, which is fine, provided that you're going for a cinematic approach that ignores real world issues. In a realistic campaign this would be a terrible house rule.<br /> <br /> Maybe add a cautionary note, like the M20 authors do, to point out that the approach above is for dramatic purposes and bears little resemblance to real world mental issues.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11490-dogstar","name":"Dogstar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11490_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"381"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-09T06:04:21-07:00","id":1303761,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dogstar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1303641-expanded-rules-for-multiple-personalities?p=1303758#post1303758\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Just a point that you seem to have fallen for the <a href=\"https://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/SplitPersonality\" target=\"_blank\">Hollywood Multiple Personality Meme</a>, which is fine, provided that you're going for a cinematic approach that ignores real world issues. In a realistic campaign this would be a terrible house rule.<br /> <br /> Maybe add a cautionary note, like the M20 authors do, to point out that the approach above is for dramatic purposes and bears little resemblance to real world mental issues.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>What I've written notes that the IRL cause of DID is trauma faced at a young age, that the alternate personalities exist as a coping mechanism, that they're essentially separate psyches. That's an accurate representation of DID, surely? The sphere/supernatural elements attached are simply a case of integrating this real-world psychological state into the fictional magickal setting. I even acknowledge that the mechanics may vary depending on how the storyteller believes DID works, since there isn't a fully accepted universal model (the bit about whether it's working with your own Mind Pattern or another Mind Pattern).<br /> <br /> The problem with Hollywood representations of DID is that they attribute it to unrealistic causes, that they make the alt personality a murderous lunatic (when alts may in fact be more mentally stable and healthy than the personality that is recognized as the &quot;main&quot; one) who is antagonistic towards their &quot;host&quot;, with lots of possession motifs.<br /> <br /> And while Hollywood often gets this condition wrong, it's worth noting that DID is going to correlate heavily with other mental and psychiatric illnesses. It's entirely rational and sensible to have one or all of the personalities have mental health issues that have resulted from abuse. Then again the &quot;main&quot; personality may not remember the abuse at all, while the &quot;alt&quot; is the one who suffered psychologically growing up, so there may be a distinct gap in mental health and quality of life. The image of the psycho-murderer alt is certainly stigmatizing and poor writing, BUT dialing it back just a few notches can give a relatively accurate image of how these personalities can manifest; one personality could easily be more prone to violence than the other, but there would be a reason for it, and they wouldn't just be an irrational insane serial killer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 04-09-2019, 09:14 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-10T04:59:57-07:00","id":1303917,"title":"","content":"As I recall (and I state this with the caveat that I am nowhere near an expert in psychology, and a request that anyone who is correct me where I am wrong) one of the main things that Hollywood gets wrong is that the separate personalities are not different identities. They are more like alternate personas or aspects that a person can slip into. It's all the same person with the same memories, just different personas. While the person may not realize they are shifting personas, it's not because they are suddenly a different person.<br /> <br /> This next bit is not psychological advice, but storytelling advice for ways of depicting psychological problems when you don't have experience with them. Most psychological disorders (that is, issues significant enough to interfere with relationships, employment, etc) are exaggerated versions of regular behavior. Everyone cycles through times where life looks better or worse, manic depressives have massive and prolonged swings between the two. Everyone has times where they are anxious about forgetting small things, people with OCD have constant and crippling anxieties about forgetting small things. Everyone mildly alters their behavior to feel more comfortable depending on situation, people with DID have much more profound shifts in order to feel comfortable, often based on cues (like memories and habits) other people around them aren't aware about.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":758,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","user":"Ramnesis","date":". 04-10-2019, 08:05 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-10T06:21:36-07:00","id":1303925,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1303641-expanded-rules-for-multiple-personalities?p=1303917#post1303917\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As I recall (and I state this with the caveat that I am nowhere near an expert in psychology, and a request that anyone who is correct me where I am wrong) one of the main things that Hollywood gets wrong is that the separate personalities are not different identities. They are more like alternate personas or aspects that a person can slip into. It's all the same person with the same memories, just different personas. While the person may not realize they are shifting personas, it's not because they are suddenly a different person.</div> </div> </div> </div>From what I'm reading, it's really more of a gradient of severity.<br /> In strong cases the &quot;Alters&quot; (Which seems to be the term for the alternate identities) indeed compartmentalize memories and sense of time away from the 'original' personality - with the orginal initially not even being aware of the Alters - so the Hollywood cliche of not remembering what happened while an alternate personality was at the front actually happens in some cases.<br /> It needs to be said however that, as you said, the Hollywood cliche displays the change of personality as too 'A completely different person'. The alternate personalities are usually not full, 'mature' personalities, but said derivations of the original - even if they actually don't necessarily identify with each other.<br /> Interestingly enough, a range of ~2 to 20(!) personalities isn't even out of the norm.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://is.gd/wodchart\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Probability Chart</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodcalc\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Dice Statistics Calculator</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodarmor\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Armor System</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage\" target=\"_blank\">cWoD Alternative Damage Roll System</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/woddamage2\" target=\"_blank\">My explanation of cWoD Damage Levels</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodstrength\" target=\"_blank\">'Interesting' Strength Attribute Stuff</a><br /> <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodboom\" target=\"_blank\">EXPLOSIVE cWoD STUFF!</a> ||| <a href=\"https://is.gd/wodheresy\" target=\"_blank\">How Technocrats don't think they are Mages</a>","edited":{"userid":6606,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","user":"Ambrosia","date":". 04-10-2019, 09:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6606-ambrosia","name":"Ambrosia","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6606_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1567"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2019-04-10T08:33:19-07:00","id":1303954,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ambrosia</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1303641-expanded-rules-for-multiple-personalities?p=1303925#post1303925\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> From what I'm reading, it's really more of a gradient of severity.<br /> In strong cases the &quot;Alters&quot; (Which seems to be the term for the alternate identities) indeed compartmentalize memories and sense of time away from the 'original' personality - with the orginal initially not even being aware of the Alters - so the Hollywood cliche of not remembering what happened while an alternate personality was at the front actually happens in some cases.<br /> It needs to be said however that, as you said, the Hollywood cliche displays the change of personality as too 'A completely different person'. The alternate personalities are usually not full, 'mature' personalities, but said derivations of the original - even if they actually don't necessarily identify with each other.<br /> Interestingly enough, a range of ~2 to 20(!) personalities isn't even out of the norm.</div> </div> </div> </div>From what I'd read, DID most commonly presents as people not remembering what happens when Alters take over; it is a *dissociation* disorder after all. There's no point retreating into your own head and letting an alter take the wheel in a stressful situation as a child (one theory about its origins is that you dissociate to protect yourself, and the alter is essentially there to experience the bad situation in your place) if you then remember the experience!<br /> <br /> Of course &quot;memory blackouts&quot; aren't universal, but a friend of mine (one of my players) works with a service user who has DID, and not only do they have several alters (who are distinct people), they even vary on gender! Thankfully they're all reasonable and relatively friendly, but their perspective on it (both the player and the service user) is that each alter is a different person who just happens to be in the same body, sharing the same 24 hours (in their own chunks).<br /> <br /> So honestly I don't think that DID is just different aspects of a single person (although it can be; calling it DID is better than calling it MPD because not everyone shows a proper split). DID can range from a severe dissociation where a different side of yourself comes out when you're under strain, up to a situation where you have 1+ other separate people inside your brain/psyche who slip in and out of control, with you having no control/memory at different times.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11620,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","user":"twig","date":". 04-10-2019, 11:38 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11620-twig","name":"twig","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11620_5.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4568"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}